{
  "unit": {
    "id": "5",
    "title": "Where, and with whom",
    "blurb": "in + ablative, cum and sine",
    "intro": "To say where someone does something, use _in_ plus the ablative: _puer in hortō ambulat_ (the boy walks in the garden). To say who is with you, or who is missing, use _cum_ (with) or _sine_ (without), which also take the ablative: _cum amīcō_, _sine amīcō_. Remember that Latin endings, not word order, do the work: the ablative ending is what tells you 'in the garden' rather than 'the garden' as an object."
  },
